# 6. Form Handling and Strict Validation

- Implement centralized reusable validation utilities.
- Validate fields on both frontend and backend.
- Never rely exclusively on frontend validation.
- Validate required fields, data types, formats, lengths, and business rules.
- Prevent invalid financial values and incorrect transaction entries.
- Validate duplicate records wherever applicable.
- Validate dependent fields and related entity selections.
- Handle whitespace-only values correctly.
- Validate date ranges and financial periods.
- Prevent invalid or unexpected numeric inputs.
- Display clear, field-specific validation messages.
- Prevent multiple form submissions.
- Disable submission buttons when processing.
- Preserve user-entered data when recoverable API errors occur.
- Avoid resetting forms unnecessarily.
- Ensure edit forms correctly populate existing data.
- Confirm unsaved changes before navigating away where appropriate.

# 7. Toast Notifications and Confirmation Dialogs

Implement a centralized customized notification and confirmation system.

### Toast Notifications
- Success notifications.
- Error notifications.
- Warning notifications.
- Information notifications.
- API failure notifications.
- Validation notifications.

### Confirmation Dialogs
Use customized confirmation dialogs for:
- Create operations when confirmation is required.
- Update operations.
- Delete operations.
- Logout.
- Cancel operations.
- Financial posting and reversal.
- Important settings changes.
- Irreversible operations.

Rules:
- Display understandable messages.
- Clearly identify the action being performed.
- Provide appropriate confirm and cancel actions.
- Prevent accidental duplicate submissions.
- Use destructive styling for irreversible actions.
- Do not use browser-native alert(), confirm(), or prompt().

# 8. Authentication and Authorization

Implement secure authentication and role-based authorization.

- Support secure login and logout.
- Implement JWT authentication with refresh-token handling.
- Store tokens securely and avoid exposing sensitive credentials.
- Implement protected routes.
- Support role-based access control (RBAC).
- Support permission-based access control.
- Control access to modules, pages, buttons, actions, and APIs.
- Enforce authorization on the backend.
- Never depend exclusively on frontend permission checks.
- Prevent unauthorized API access.
- Handle expired sessions correctly.
- Implement secure password policies.
- Implement password reset and account recovery.
- Support session expiration and revocation.
- Record important authentication activities.

### Superadmin Access

- Superadmin must have unrestricted access to all application modules and administrative functionality.
- Superadmin must be exempt from subscription and free-trial restrictions.
- Implement a secure superadmin account initialization process.
- Provide an optional superadmin auto-login mechanism for development and testing environments only.
- Never enable automatic production login using hardcoded credentials or publicly accessible bypasses.
- Production superadmin access must require secure authentication.
- Maintain a complete audit trail of superadmin activities.
- Superadmin must be able to manage organizations, users, roles, permissions, plans, subscriptions, and system settings.

# 9. Role and Permission Management

- Implement dynamic role management.
- Implement granular permission management.
- Support module-level and action-level permissions.
- Support Create, Read, Update, Delete, Export, Print, Approve, and other required permissions.
- Allow permissions to be assigned to roles.
- Allow users to have appropriate role assignments.
- Support organization-specific roles.
- Ensure permission changes take effect correctly.
- Prevent unauthorized users from accessing restricted functionality.
- Display only the permitted navigation items and actions.
- Validate all permissions on the backend.
- Maintain permission change history.

# 10. SaaS Architecture and Multi-Tenancy

Build the application as a multi-tenant SaaS product.

- Support multiple organizations or tenants.
- Each organization must have isolated financial records.
- Implement tenant identification and secure tenant resolution.
- Ensure every tenant-specific query is scoped correctly.
- Prevent cross-tenant data access.
- Enforce tenant isolation at the backend and database levels.
- Support organization registration and onboarding.
- Support organization-specific configurations.
- Support organization-specific users, roles, and permissions.
- Support organization-specific financial settings.
- Support tenant-level audit trails.
- Support tenant suspension and reactivation.
- Avoid exposing one organization's information to another.
- Ensure background jobs and scheduled tasks also respect tenant boundaries.

# 11. Subscription and Free Trial Management

Implement a complete SaaS subscription management system.

- Support free trial subscriptions.
- Support multiple subscription plans.
- Support monthly and yearly billing.
- Support plan upgrades and downgrades.
- Support subscription renewal.
- Support trial expiration.
- Support subscription expiration.
- Support payment pending and payment failure states.
- Support cancellation and reactivation.
- Support plan-specific module access.
- Support user limits and storage limits.
- Support feature-based restrictions.
- Display subscription status and remaining trial days.
- Provide subscription renewal reminders.
- Implement payment gateway integration securely.
- Verify payment gateway webhooks.
- Prevent duplicate payment processing.
- Maintain complete payment and subscription history.
- Implement configurable grace periods and account restrictions.
- Ensure subscription enforcement happens on the backend.

### Subscription Exemptions

- Superadmin must be completely exempt from subscription and trial restrictions.
- All other roles and users must follow their organization's subscription rules.
- Do not bypass subscription restrictions through frontend manipulation.
- Ensure permissions and subscription entitlements are evaluated independently.

# 12. Finance and Accounting Integrity

Financial accuracy is a critical requirement.

- Use appropriate decimal precision for monetary calculations.
- Never use floating-point arithmetic for financial calculations.
- Use decimal types in .NET and appropriate database decimal/numeric types.
- Define consistent currency precision and rounding rules.
- Validate debit and credit entries.
- Enforce double-entry accounting rules wherever applicable.
- Ensure total debit equals total credit before posting balanced journal entries.
- Use database transactions for critical financial operations.
- Prevent duplicate voucher posting.
- Prevent unauthorized modifications to posted transactions.
- Support controlled reversal and cancellation workflows.
- Maintain financial period validations.
- Prevent transactions in locked accounting periods.
- Maintain opening balances and closing balances accurately.
- Implement proper exchange-rate handling for multi-currency transactions.
- Preserve original transaction values and exchange rates.
- Maintain complete transaction references.
- Implement reliable financial reports.
- Ensure report totals match underlying transaction data.
- Maintain audit history for financial modifications.
- Never silently change historical financial records.

# 13. Database Management and Optimization

- Maintain a normalized and consistent database structure where appropriate.
- Use appropriate primary keys and foreign keys.
- Define proper unique constraints.
- Create indexes for frequently searched and filtered columns.
- Avoid unnecessary database queries.
- Prevent N+1 query problems.
- Optimize joins, filtering, sorting, and pagination.
- Use server-side pagination for large datasets.
- Avoid loading unnecessary records.
- Use database transactions for critical operations.
- Implement safe database migrations.
- Never delete or modify production data without proper safeguards.
- Implement backup and restoration procedures.
- Maintain database integrity.
- Review slow queries and execution plans.
- Use connection pooling appropriately.

# 14. Performance Optimization

- Avoid unnecessary API calls and database operations.
- Avoid unnecessary component re-rendering.
- Implement efficient caching strategies.
- Use lazy loading for large modules.
- Split bundles appropriately.
- Optimize large tables and datasets.
- Use pagination and virtualization where required.
- Optimize images, icons, and static assets.
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies.
- Avoid memory leaks.
- Clean up subscriptions and event listeners.
- Avoid expensive calculations during every render.
- Optimize backend queries and API response sizes.
- Implement suitable loading strategies.
- Monitor frontend performance and backend response times.
- Ensure the application remains responsive with large datasets.

# 15. Error Handling, Logging and Monitoring

- Implement centralized frontend error handling.
- Implement centralized backend exception handling.
- Use structured logging.
- Maintain separate development and production logging configurations.
- Never expose stack traces or sensitive information to end users.
- Log important API failures.
- Log authentication and authorization failures.
- Log critical financial operations.
- Implement application health checks.
- Monitor application availability.
- Monitor slow API endpoints.
- Capture unexpected production errors.
- Implement appropriate error recovery mechanisms.
- Avoid unnecessary console.log statements in production.
- Never log passwords, access tokens, payment secrets, or sensitive financial information.

# 16. Security Standards

- Follow OWASP security recommendations.
- Validate and sanitize all external inputs.
- Prevent SQL injection.
- Prevent cross-site scripting (XSS).
- Implement appropriate CSRF protection where applicable.
- Configure CORS securely.
- Use HTTPS.
- Secure authentication and authorization endpoints.
- Apply rate limiting to sensitive endpoints.
- Protect against brute-force login attempts.
- Secure file uploads and downloads.
- Validate uploaded file types, extensions, and sizes.
- Store secrets in secure configuration systems.
- Avoid exposing internal server details.
- Apply least-privilege principles.
- Protect tenant-specific data.
- Implement secure password hashing.
- Maintain secure session management.

