- Verify production environment configurations.
- Ensure application logs and monitoring are available.

# 22. AI Coding Assistant Rules

When using Cursor AI or any other AI coding assistant:

- Always inspect the existing project structure before making changes.
- Understand the existing business logic before modifying code.
- Do not blindly rewrite existing working components.
- Do not create duplicate files, components, services, or APIs.
- Reuse existing shared components and utilities.
- Follow the project's existing naming conventions.
- Use JSX files for frontend implementation.
- Do not introduce TypeScript without explicit approval.
- Do not use mock or static data when real API integration is required.
- Do not assume API response structures; inspect the actual backend contracts.
- Never invent API endpoints, DTO fields, database columns, or business rules.
- Check imports and dependencies after every modification.
- Fix build errors before considering the task complete.
- Execute relevant tests after modifications.
- Review the final code for unnecessary logic and duplication.
- Report changed files, implemented functionality, tests executed, and known limitations.
- Ask for clarification when essential business requirements are unclear.
- Do not mark incomplete features as completed.

